    "A": "Research an algorithmic improvement: enumerate boundary pairs along the shorter matrix dimension, incrementally accumulate sums along the longer dimension, and count 1D subarrays with a prefix-frequency table. Arrange data to preserve useful locality where practical. Compare against the current column-pair implementation on rectangular and square inputs. Preserve signed values, all valid dimensions and k values, and 64-bit sums and answer; do not assume any particular test shape or values.",
    "B": "Research frequency-table alternatives within the existing stripe-counting algorithm so the data-structure effect can be measured separately from algorithm changes. Compare the current unordered_map approach with a carefully bounded, collision-safe flat hash table using per-sweep reset/generation tags; consider coordinate compression as another reference. Key by full signed prefix sums, handle negative sums and k correctly, and keep 64-bit arithmetic for sums and the total count. Return a standalone, correctness-checked candidate and measurements.",
    "C": "Research parallelizing independent boundary-pair work for the four-core ARM64 target, using only the unchanged compiler/build configuration and already-supported parallel facilities. Keep each worker's accumulation and frequency-table state private and combine counts safely; avoid shared-map contention and assess parallel overhead on small workloads as well as scaling on larger ones. Preserve a correct serial path where parallel overhead is not worthwhile. No GPU work is indicated by this target.",
    "G": "Integrate only candidates that are independently correct and measured. Compare each candidate and each proposed combination under the same run conditions; do not assume speedups compose. Prefer the fastest measured correct result, retain full correctness for every valid input, and do not change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions. Keep the integration focused and report which measured candidate or combination was selected.",
    "analysis": "The supplied source always pairs columns and scans rows, giving O(M·N²) work with repeated unordered_map operations. Pairing along the shorter dimension can reduce the asymptotic work to O(min(M,N)²·max(M,N)); separate histogram and parallelism experiments can isolate other opportunities. The only automatic observation is a correct baseline on 4 cases, with mean time 0.2500246996060014 seconds and score 0.5 on ARM64 with 4 CPU cores and no GPU. There are no prior candidate or interaction measurements, so gains and combinations must be measured rather than presumed."
